  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on province-level panel data, which may mask finer sub-provincial or municipal variations in income distribution and expressway accessibility.",
      "what": "This study analyzed panel data from 30 Chinese provinces between 2010 and 2023 to evaluate how expressway development affects the urban-rural income gap. Using threshold regression and Geographically and Temporally Weighted Regression (GTWR), the analysis measures how regional factor-carrying capacity, represented by population density and economic development, alters this relationship. The findings show that while expressway expansion generally reduces income inequality through improved logistics and labor mobility, it actually widens the income gap in regions with low factor-carrying capacity by concentrating production factors in core areas.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that the economic distribution effects of transport infrastructure are non-linear and highly dependent on local development levels, reconciling previous contradictory findings by showing that infrastructure expansion only reduces inequality after a region passes a specific development threshold."
    }
  },
